t définitions suivants s?--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe photodegradation of polyethylene and polypropylene in the presence and absence of added titanium dioxide - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0014305781900501)\nciteturn35search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; The durabilities of polyethylene and polypropylene, in the presence and absence of added titanium dioxide, towards photodegradation have been assessed by the rate of oxygen absorption upon irradiation, measurement of photo-induced carbonyl compound and hydroperoxide formation using i.r. spectroscopy, and by measuring the change in tensile properties caused by irradiation.\n\n## European Polymer Journal\n\nVolume 17, Issue 2, 1981, Pages 163-167\n\n# The photodegradation of polyethylene and polypropylene in the presence and absence of added titanium dioxide\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/0014-3057(81)90050-1Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe durabilities of polyethylene and polypropylene, in the presence and absence of added titanium dioxide, towards photodegradation have been assessed by the rate of oxygen absorption upon irradiation, measurement of photo-induced carbonyl compound and hydroperoxide formation using i.r. spectroscopy, and by measuring the change in tensile properties caused by irradiation. Oxygen absorption measurements are relatively insensitive and this method appears to be unreliable. Measurement of carbonyl compound and hydroperoxide formation by i.r. spectroscopy is suitable for polyethylene but not for polypropylene. The most sensitive method of following degradation appears to be the measuring of change in tensile properties. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInfluence of colourants on environmental degradation of plastic litter. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38432345/)\nciteturn35search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; A static experimental exposure of differently coloured polypropylene bottle tops from the same manufacturer to a moderate climate over 3 years showed that black, white and silver plastics were almost unaffected whereas the specific blue, green and especially red pigments used in this study were significantly degraded. ... It appears that carbon and titanium dioxide colourants protect the HDPE polymer from photolytic degradation.\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nPlastic degradation and the resultant production of microplastics has an important effect on the environment and fauna across the world. This paper shows that the colourant incorporated into plastic formulations has a significant effect on the stability of plastics. A static experimental exposure of differently coloured polypropylene bottle tops from the same manufacturer to a moderate climate over 3 years showed that black, white and silver plastics were almost unaffected whereas the specific blue, green and especially red pigments used in this study were significantly degraded. The second part of the study collected littered HDPE plastic containers from a remote South African beach and analysed their condition as a function of the given manufacturing date stamp. Most items were black or white and samples up to 45 years old were found with relatively little environmental degradation other than mild abrasion. It appears that carbon and titanium dioxide colourants protect the HDPE polymer from photolytic degradation. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdvanced TiO2-based catalysts for polypropylene degradation in aquatic media - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40974772/)\nciteturn35search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Advanced TiO_{2}-based catalysts for polypropylene degradation in aquatic media ... Polypropylene (PP), together with low-density polyethylene (PE), are the most common MPs found in the environment, being released in an amount of approximately 112 million tons per year.\nJ Environ Manage. 2026 Apr 15;404:129332. doi: 10.1016/j.jenvman.2026.129332. Epub 2026 Mar 14. J Environ Manage. 2026. PMID: 41833461 No abstract available.\n\n## Abstract\n\nPlastics are polluting our environment and oceans with a large number and variety of organic and inorganic compounds that arise as a consequence of the current industrial development. Every year we release to the environment millions of tons of plastics whose total elimination may take centuries. The exponential increase in the production of this material is causing a serious environmental problem, resulting in the generation of a large amount of this waste. Microplastics (MPs) are released into the environment in different ways, but one of the most important is through effluents from wastewater treatment plants, since no specific method is used to eliminate or degrade these MPs before they are discharged into the environment. Polypropylene (PP), together with low-density polyethylene (PE), are the most common MPs found in the environment, being released in an amount of approximately 112 million tons per year. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPolypropylene Degradation on Co-Rotating Twin-Screw Extruders - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37177327/)\nciteturn35search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The material degradation of polypropylene (PP) on a 28 mm twin-screw extruder has already been studied and modeled at Kunststofftechnik Paderborn. ... Furthermore, polypropylene compounds with titanium dioxide as well as carbon fibers were considered on a 28 mm extruder.\n## Abstract\n\nNowadays, usable plastic materials with defined properties are created by blending additives into the base polymer. This is the main task of compounding on co-rotating twin-screw extruders. The thermal and mechanical stress occurring in the process leads to a mostly irreversible damage to the material. Consequently, the properties of the polymer melt and the subsequent product are affected. The material degradation of polypropylene (PP) on a 28 mm twin-screw extruder has already been studied and modeled at Kunststofftechnik Paderborn. In this work, the transferability of the previous results to other machine sizes and polypropylene compounds were investigated experimentally. Therefore, pure polypropylene was processed with screw diameters of 25 mm and 45 mm. Furthermore, polypropylene compounds with titanium dioxide as well as carbon fibers were considered on a 28 mm extruder. In the course of the evaluation of the pure polypropylene, the melt flow rates of the samples were measured and the molar masses were calculated on this basis. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnhancing the impact and flexural strength of PP/LLDPE/TiO2/SEBS nano-composites by using Taguchi methodology - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0266353816301531)\nciteturn35search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The present study was an attempt to maximize the impact and flexural strength of polypropylene/linear low-density polyethylene/nano-titanium dioxide/styrene-ethylene-butylene-styrene (PP/LLDPE/TiO_{2}/SEBS) nano-composites simultaneously by optimizing the LLDPE, TiO_{2}, and SEBS. ... Blending of PP and LLDPE has been attempted to achieve improvement in chemical, physical and mechanical properties, where PP ensures good processability and insensitivity to moisture, while LLDPE contributes impact properties.\n\n## Composites Science and Technology\n\nDate: 6 June 2016\n\nPages: 61-69\n\nVolume: Volume 129\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Enhancing the impact and flexural strength of PP/LLDPE/TiO_{2}/SEBS nano-composites by using Taguchi methodology\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Mohsen Ayaz ^{a}, Sajjad Daneshpayeh ^{b}, Ali Noroozi ^{b}\n\n\n10.1016/j.compscitech.2016.04.015\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe present study was an attempt to maximize the impact and flexural strength of polypropylene/linear low-density polyethylene/nano-titanium dioxide/styrene-ethylene-butylene-styrene (PP/LLDPE/TiO_{2}/SEBS) nano-composites simultaneously by optimizing the LLDPE, TiO_{2}, and SEBS. An L9 orthogonal array of Taguchi method was used for the experimental design to extract the optimal level of parameters by signal to noise (S/N) ratio. The S/N ratios results were then investigated by grey relational analysis (GRA) in order to determine optimal condition of the impact and flexural strength. Simultaneous enhancement of the impact and flexural strength was achieved by 33.85% under optimal condition, i.e. 40 wt% LLDPE, 2 wt% TiO_{2} and 6 wt% SEBS. The effectiveness of the proposed approach was demonstrated through confirmation tests at the optimal levels of parameters.\n\n## Keywords\n\nPP/LLDPE/TiO_{2}/SEBS nano-composite\n\n;\n\nImpact strength\n\n;\n\nFlexural strength\n\n;\n\nTaguchi method\n\n;\n\nGrey relational analysis\n\n\n## Introduction\n\nPolypropylene (PP) has been used as the matrix material in a commercial form due to its many good properties, such as light weight, easy processability, relatively low cost, excellent heat distortion temperature and its durability [1], [2]. However, PP has certain drawbacks that limit its usage in some applications. One of these drawbacks is its poor impact strength especially at low temperature due to the inherently high glass transition temperature (Tg) and high crystallinity [3]. Adding flexible polymers such as linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is one of the most efficient methods to toughen PP for different high performance applications. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPredicting of mechanical properties of PP/LLDPE/TiO2 nano-composites by response surface methodology - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1359836815005156)\nciteturn35search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; ### Effect of titanium dioxide on the properties of polyethylene/TiO_{2} nano-composites ... ### Nonisothermal melt-crystallization and mechanical properties of titanium (IV) oxide nanoparticle-filled isotactic polypropylene\n## Section snippets\n\n## Materials\n\nPolypropylene (PP-Z30S, MFR-25, 230 °C, 2.16 kg) and linear low density polyethylene (LLDPE-0209, MFR-0.9, 190 °C, 2.16 kg, density 0.920 g/ml) were supplied from Arak Petrochemical Company, Iran. The nano TiO_{2} rutile structure with 30 nm average size and 4.23 g/cm^{3} density was purchased from Iranian Nano-materials Pioneers (INP). In addition, KRATON polymer type G, namely styrene–ethylene/butylene–styrene (SEBS) grade 6110 from Dynasol Company, was used as a coupling agent.\n\n## Sample preparation\n\nCompounding was\n\n## Effect of nano-particle on tensile strength\n\nIn order to find the significant parameters affecting the PP/LLDPE/TiO_{2} nano-composites, Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) was performed. ANOVA is a statistical procedure that can draw a set of essential conclusions based on the analysis of experimental data. In an ANOVA table, the sum of squares (SS) is the data used to estimate the Fisher's variance ratio (F-value). F-value is the measure of variation in the data about the mean. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMitigating UV‑C Degradation in Polypropylene Using Hybrid TiO2/Few-Layer Graphene/Photostabilizer Systems - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41244454/)\nciteturn35search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: last week; # Mitigating UV‑C Degradation in Polypropylene Using Hybrid TiO_{2}/Few-Layer Graphene/Photostabilizer Systems ... Different photostabilizers can be used to enhance polymer resistance against UV, such as UV screeners like titanium dioxide rutile (TiO_{2}), radical scavengers like Irganox B215, a commercial H-donor and peroxide scavenger, and, more recently, graphene and its derivatives like few-layer graphene (FLG).\n## Abstract\n\nPolypropylene (PP) is a versatile thermoplastic widely used in industrial fields. In medical devices, PP is preferred for applications that involve storing or coming into contact with biological fluids. However, when exposed to UV-C, commonly used as a cleansing tool in hospitals, PP undergoes a photodegradation process, resulting in chain scissions and branching reactions that impact the material properties and lifespan. Different photostabilizers can be used to enhance polymer resistance against UV, such as UV screeners like titanium dioxide rutile (TiO_{2}), radical scavengers like Irganox B215, a commercial H-donor and peroxide scavenger, and, more recently, graphene and its derivatives like few-layer graphene (FLG). Graphene has gained attention as an alternative photostabilizer in polymers for having different types of UV photoprotection mechanisms, such as UV absorbers/screeners and radical scavengers. In this context, this study aimed to evaluate the effectiveness of FLG and Irganox B215 as radical scavengers in combination with TiO_{2} to minimize the effect of parallel radical formation (ROS) from TiO_{2} electron-hole reactions and from the PP photodegradation autocatalytic cycle. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDynamic investigations of opto-mechanical properties of iPP/TiO2 nanocomposites fibres using fringe analysis techniques - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0030402620305179)\nciteturn35search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; These materials have optical, mechanical, electrical, electrochemical and thermal properties differ remarkably from that of the component materials. ... These samples consist of isotactic polypropylene (iPP) as a bulk material and titanium oxide nanoparticles (2% TiO_{2}) as additives to constitute iPP/ TiO_{2} nanocomposites.\n\n## Optik\n\nVolume 212, June 2020, 164683\n\n# Dynamic investigations of opto-mechanical properties of iPP/TiO_{2} nanocomposites fibres using fringe analysis techniques\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijleo.2020.164683Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present an accurate and fast in-situ investigation of opto-mechanical properties of iPP/TiO_{2} nanocomposites fibres using a Mach–Zehnder interferometer. The rotation band pass filter approach, autofocusing approach and other approaches are used to overcome the error sources that accompaniments the in-situ investigation of opto-mechanical properties of nanocomposites fibres. The autofocusing approach is applied to overcome the issues which are produced from the heterogeneous distribution of the nanoparticle modifiers throughout the bulk of fibre. According to this approach a meaningful phase information from interference patterns suffered from axially tilted (contains a mix of foci and out of foci parts in the same frame) is extracted. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternational Standards in process (https://www.iso.org/files/live/sites/isoorg/files/news/magazine/ISOupdate/EN/2026/iso_update_202605_en.pdf)\nciteturn35search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Plastics — Ion exchange resin — Part 6: Determi- ... Plastics — Evaluation of the dispersibility and ... Natural gas-Upstream area-Requirements and test ... ISO/CD 16900-1 ... ISO/CD 16900-4\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternational Standards in process (https://www.iso.org/files/live/sites/isoorg/files/news/magazine/ISOupdate/EN/2026/iso_update_202604_en.pdf)\nciteturn35search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Plastics — U